    {
      "id": "qpyoAO17doKRSAQVRqt3w",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -7655.496132482318,
      "y": 3826.395449764672,
      "width": 5098.8212890625,
      "height": 3253.333333333343,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bkl",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1989473289,
      "version": 931,
      "versionNonce": 1502465795,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1774118033108,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Java Concurrency\n    - Parallel, Concurrent and Distributed Programming \n        in Java\n    - Virtual Threads\n\nNetworking\n    - OSI & TCP/UDP\n    - RPC / Serialization\n\nOS\n    - CAP Theorem\n\nResources\n        - Java Concurrency in Practice (Brian Goetz)\n        - Designing Data-Intensive Applications\n        - Distributed Systems by Maarten van Steen and Andrew Tanenbaum\n\n        - MIT 6.5840\n        - Parallel, Concurrent, and Distributed Programming in Java (coursera)\n        - Java Multithreading (youtube)\n        - Distributed Systems by Martin Kleppmann\n\nPractice\n        - A Distributed Key-Value Store: Use Java and implement a consensus algorithm\n            like Raft or Paxos from scratch (don't use a library).\n        - A Real-time Stream Processor: Use Apache Flink or Kafka to process parallel data streams.\n        - The \"Dining Philosophers\" in 2026: Re-implement classic concurrency problems using Java Virtual Threads (Project Loom)\n            to show you are up-to-date with modern Java performance.\n\nRoadmap\n    https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/system-design/what-are-the-requirements-to-learn-distributed-systems/\n",
      "fontSize": 81.33333333333357,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Java Concurrency\n    - Parallel, Concurrent and Distributed Programming \n        in Java\n    - Virtual Threads\n\nNetworking\n    - OSI & TCP/UDP\n    - RPC / Serialization\n\nOS\n    - CAP Theorem\n\nResources\n        - Java Concurrency in Practice (Brian Goetz)\n        - Designing Data-Intensive Applications\n        - Distributed Systems by Maarten van Steen and Andrew Tanenbaum\n\n        - MIT 6.5840\n        - Parallel, Concurrent, and Distributed Programming in Java (coursera)\n        - Java Multithreading (youtube)\n        - Distributed Systems by Martin Kleppmann\n\nPractice\n        - A Distributed Key-Value Store: Use Java and implement a consensus algorithm\n            like Raft or Paxos from scratch (don't use a library).\n        - A Real-time Stream Processor: Use Apache Flink or Kafka to process parallel data streams.\n        - The \"Dining Philosophers\" in 2026: Re-implement classic concurrency problems using Java Virtual Threads (Project Loom)\n            to show you are up-to-date with modern Java performance.\n\nRoadmap\n    https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/system-design/what-are-the-requirements-to-learn-distributed-systems/\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
